  "context": [
    "The Wagon R has been a fixture in Maruti Suzuki's India lineup since 1999, and the current tall-boy generation, on sale since 2019 and lightly updated in 2022, continues to lean on the formula that built its reputation: maximum cabin space for its footprint, a familiar silhouette, and Maruti's unmatched Arena service network. That network density is the single biggest lever on Wagon R resale, since owners in smaller towns can get parts and service almost anywhere, which keeps buyer confidence high even on higher-mileage examples.",
    "Two petrol engines are sold alongside each other: the smaller 1.0-litre K10C three-cylinder on base trims and the 1.2-litre K12N four-cylinder from VXi upward, with the 1.2 also offering Maruti's AGS automated-manual gearbox. In the used market, 1.2-litre cars with AGS have gained ground over the last two years as city buyers increasingly prioritise an automatic for stop-go traffic, narrowing what used to be a wider price gap with manual variants.",
    "Factory-fitted CNG, offered on LXi and VXi trims, is a meaningful share of Wagon R sales in Delhi NCR, Gujarat, and parts of Maharashtra, and it resells at a premium over equivalent petrol in those markets because running-cost-conscious used buyers actively seek it out. Outside CNG-infrastructure-heavy states, the premium narrows or disappears, and buyers show a mild preference for straightforward petrol cars instead.",
    "Because the Wagon R competes directly with the Swift and Celerio for the same used-car budget, its resale curve tracks the broader Maruti hatchback segment closely, trailing the Swift by a small margin at every age band. The tall-boy shape has slightly narrower appeal among younger, style-conscious buyers who dominate hatchback resale demand, even though practicality-focused family buyers continue to value it highly."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Maruti Wagon R in India?",
      "a": "A well-kept Wagon R retains close to 73% of its ex-showroom price after two years and around 51% after five years, based on transaction data across Indian metros as of September 2026. A 2022 VXi 1.2 petrol with 25,000-50,000 km typically trades in a band of roughly ₹4.1-4.7 lakh depending on city and condition."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does CNG improve Wagon R resale value?",
      "a": "Yes, in CNG-strong markets. Factory-fitted CNG Wagon R units trade at a premium over equivalent petrol in Delhi NCR and Ahmedabad, both of which carry a positive city variance in this data, because fuel-cost-conscious buyers actively search for factory CNG. In cities with thinner CNG infrastructure, the premium shrinks toward parity with petrol."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the 1.2-litre AGS automatic worth more at resale than the manual?",
      "a": "AGS variants have closed most of the price gap with manual Wagon Rs over the past two years as automatic-transmission demand has risen sharply among city buyers. At equivalent age and mileage, an AGS Wagon R typically commands a modest premium over a manual VXi or ZXi, though it narrows the gap rather than reversing it."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Wagon R depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The Wagon R loses roughly 16% of its value in year one and another 9-10 percentage points in year two, settling into a steadier 6-7% annual decline from year three onward. Five-year retention of about 51% is roughly in line with the hatchback segment average of about 50% used here."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Wagon R?",
      "a": "Delhi NCR and Ahmedabad currently trade at about +3% above the national mid, driven by strong CNG demand, while Kolkata trades at roughly -3%, reflecting softer used-hatchback demand in that market."
    }
  ],
